tiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nThe second fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nand the reciprocal of this fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 3 4 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {4} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} = \\dfrac {3 \\times 5} {4 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nSolving this problem using fraction models gives the same result:\n\n![](images/de/de4e554717f886b8e4b289a50d19e57f1b30383f0d7f4bf7b85476a57a442706.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144078 #edb/ref 1065 8601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144078 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8a2b2b99-40dc-5215-997c-152bda4c83ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144078 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
